.TH _PRINTF 3 "March 2026" "_printf project" "Custom Library Functions"
.SH NAME
_printf \- produces output according to a format

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.B int _printf(const char *format, ...);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf
function produces output according to a format string and writes it to the
standard output.

The format string is composed of ordinary characters and conversion
specifiers. Ordinary characters are printed as they appear. A conversion
specifier begins with the percent character
.B %
and is followed by a character that determines how the corresponding argument
is printed.

This implementation of
.B _printf
supports the following conversion specifiers:
.TP
.B %c
Prints a single character.
.TP
.B %s
Prints a string.
.TP
.B %%
Prints a percent sign.
.TP
.B %d
Prints a decimal integer.
.TP
.B %i
Prints an integer.

If the character following
.B %
is not a valid supported conversion specifier, the function prints the percent
sign followed by that character.

If the format string is
.B NULL
or if a percent sign appears at the end of the format string with no following
specifier, the function returns
.BR -1 .

.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful return,
.B _printf
returns the number of characters printed, excluding the terminating null byte.

On error, the function returns
.BR -1 .

.SH EXAMPLES
.B _printf("Hello %s\\n", "world");
.PP
Output:
.PP
Hello world

.PP
.B _printf("Character: %c\\n", 'A');
.PP
Output:
.PP
Character: A

.PP
.B _printf("Value: %d\\n", 1234);
.PP
Output:
.PP
Value: 1234

.PP
.B _printf("%K\\n");
.PP
Output:
.PP
%K

.SH FILES
.B main.h
Header file containing the prototype of
.BR _printf ,
as well as the prototypes of helper functions.

